## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates understanding of lock-free inter-process communication under simple multicore constraints and cache coherence protocols. It distinguishes candidates who know the mechanics of atomics from those who understand visibility and ordering guarantees.

## Key Concepts
- Lock-free ring buffers with atomic indices
- Acquire/release semantics for safe cross-core visibility
- Wrap-around via power-of-two masking
- Contention handling and backoff strategies

## Code Example
```c
#define SIZE 1024  // power of two
#define MASK (SIZE - 1)

_Atomic size_t head = 0;
_Atomic size_t tail = 0;
type_t buffer[SIZE];

void enqueue(type_t value) {
    size_t h = atomic_fetch_add(&head, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
    buffer[h & MASK] = value;
    atomic_thread_fence(memory_order_release);
}

type_t dequeue(void) {
    size_t t = atomic_load(&tail, memory_order_acquire);
    type_t value = buffer[t & MASK];
    atomic_fetch_add(&tail, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
    return value;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Designing a per-process memory quota linked to scheduling ensures predictable memory pressure control without relying on OOM killers. It tests integration between memory accounting, page fault paths, and the scheduler's runqueues, plus correctness under contention.

## Key Concepts

- Per-task memory accounting and quotas
- Page fault handling and page cache interactions
- Scheduler runqueues and throttling paths
- Burst handling, epoch resets, cross-core fairness

## Code Example

```c
// Minimal quota accounting sketch
typedef struct {
  size_t quota;   // bytes allowed
  size_t usage;   // bytes currently used
  unsigned long epoch; // quota reset period
  struct task_struct *task;
} mem_quota_t;

// Called on page fault path
static int quota_account_page(struct task_struct *task, struct page *page)
{
  mem_quota_t *mq = task->mem_quota;
  size_t page_sz = PAGE_SIZE;

  // Atomically update usage
  if (atomic_fetch_add(&mq->usage, page_sz) + page_sz > mq->quota) {
    // Over quota: throttle the task
    task->sched_info.time_slice /= 2; // reduce timeslice
    enqueue_task_low_prio(task);      // move to low-priority queue
    return -ENOMEM;
  }
  return 0;
}
```
